infographic compare-binary-horizontal-underline-text-vs
data
  title Interface ownership: provider vs consumer
  desc DIP is not just an interface — it is who controls the abstraction
  compares
    - label Provider owns it
      children
        - label Provider can change it
          desc Order team renames, splits, or moves it — every consumer breaks.
        - label Abstraction is a detail
          desc Violates DIP second half: the abstraction depends on the low-level module.
        - label Arrow not inverted
          desc Billing still imports from order — the dependency points the wrong way.
        - label An interface, but a fiction
          desc The interface exists, yet ownership never moved — no real decoupling.
    - label Consumer owns it
      children
        - label Consumer controls change
          desc IOrderPort changes only when billing own requirements change.
        - label Details depend on abstraction
          desc Order repository implements billing contract — DIP second half holds.
        - label Arrow inverts
          desc The low-level provider now depends on the high-level consumer boundary.
        - label Compile-time boundary
          desc Billing sees only IOrderPort; the compiler enforces the line.
theme
  palette #f43f5e #14b8a6
